From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pete O_o <pmb@meta.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:55:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hwmontempsensor: discard out-of-range readings

Discard hwmon readings which fall outside the sensor's min/max range
(-128..127 C for temperature sensors), counting them as read errors
instead.

Reason:
* Broadcom Thor Ultra NICs (BCM57708/BCM57704) report their core
  temperature by emulating a TMP421.  While the NIC's management
  co-processor runs POST its SMBus slave is not yet enabled, so the
  BMC reads back 0xFF from the idle bus and the tmp421 driver decodes
  it as 191.938 C.  This lasts up to ~5s after an AC cycle and <1s
  after a DC cycle (Broadcom KB article 27559).
* A single such reading exceeds every upper threshold on the NIC
  sensor, logging spurious threshold events.

The same approach was taken upstream for SmbpbiSensor [1].  A single
bad read leaves the last good value in place; five in a row mark the
sensor non-functional until a valid reading arrives.

[1]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/83578

Signed-off-by: Pete O_o <pmb@meta.com>
---
 src/hwmon-temp/HwmonTempSensor.cpp | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/hwmon-temp/HwmonTempSensor.cpp b/src/hwmon-temp/HwmonTempSensor.cpp
index 2957b09..50520f5 100644
--- a/src/hwmon-temp/HwmonTempSensor.cpp
+++ b/src/hwmon-temp/HwmonTempSensor.cpp
@@ -194,7 +194,21 @@ void HwmonTempSensor::handleResponse(const boost::system::error_code& err,
         }
         else
         {
-            updateValue((nvalue + offsetValue) * scaleValue);
+            double reading = (nvalue + offsetValue) * scaleValue;
+            // Some devices answer reads with garbage while initialising, e.g.
+            // a Broadcom Thor NIC emulating a TMP421 returns 0xFF during its
+            // POST, which decodes as 191.938 C.  Discard readings outside the
+            // sensor's range rather than asserting thresholds on them.
+            if (reading > maxValue || reading < minValue)
+            {
+                lg2::error("Sensor '{NAME}' reading out of range: {VALUE}",
+                           "NAME", name, "VALUE", reading);
+                incrementError();
+            }
+            else
+            {
+                updateValue(reading);
+            }
         }
     }
     else
